@meta
id: 02-09
title: Undoing several changes at once
chapter: 02
type: lesson
skills: undo, redo
par: 55
minutes: 5
vscode: holding Ctrl+Z and hoping
open: release-plan.md

@brief
Almost every normal mode command takes a number in front of it, and it
means what you would guess: do this that many times. `u` is no exception.
`3u` undoes three changes. `2` then `Ctrl-R` redoes two.

That sounds like a small convenience. It is really about precision —
`3u` lands on a state you chose, where holding `u` down lands wherever
your reflexes let go.

The release plan is being ticked off as the deploy runs. In order:

  1. Append `: done` to each of the four bullets, top to bottom, as four
     separate edits.

  2. The deploy is rolled back at the announcement step. Take the last
     three ticks off in one command.

  3. It restarts, and migration, feature flag and smoke tests all pass
     again. Put two ticks back in one command.

Save. The first three bullets end with `: done`, the announcement does
not.

@keys
| `3u` | undo three changes |
| `2` `Ctrl-R` | redo two changes |
| `u` `Ctrl-R` | one at a time |

@hints
Four separate edits means four entries in the undo history, so the counts
line up with the bullets exactly.
--
The count goes before the letter: `3u`, not `u3`. For redo, type the
count and then press `Ctrl-R`.
--
After `3u` only the Migration tick survives. `2` then `Ctrl-R` replays
the feature flag and smoke test ticks, in that order.

@solution
    jj $ a : done <Esc>
    j $ a : done <Esc>
    j $ a : done <Esc>
    j $ a : done <Esc>
    3u
    2<Ctrl-R>
    ZZ

`3u` is one byte shorter than `uuu`, which is not the reason to use it.
The reason is that you can count the changes you regret, then undo
exactly that many, and know where you have landed without reading the
file to check.

Counts are not special to undo. `3j` moves down three lines, `3dd`
deletes three lines, `3.` repeats an edit three times. You have just met
the general rule a chapter early, on the safest possible command.

If you ever lose track of how far back you have gone, vim tells you.
After each `u` the message line reports the change number and how long
ago it happened: `3 changes; before #4  12 seconds ago`. Read it instead
of guessing.

@start release-plan.md
# 2.14 release plan

- Migration
- Feature flag
- Smoke tests
- Announcement

@expected release-plan.md
# 2.14 release plan

- Migration: done
- Feature flag: done
- Smoke tests: done
- Announcement
